in a group chat with friends you can kind of tell when someone is about to type even before they

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:13.420
send anything. You watch the little dots or just sense the rhythm of the conversation. Now imagine

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:20.280
that same awareness but for sound. The AI listens to the stream of audio coming in and every fraction of

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:25.900
a second makes a tiny decision. Is this a good moment for me to reply or should I keep listening?

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:32.280
It does not wait for a long pause like older systems did. Think of the audio like a long line of cars

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:38.260
on a highway. Instead of treating the whole recording as one big chunk, it breaks the incoming sound

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:44.280
into tiny slices and checks each one. That way it can react to things like someone clearing their

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:50.020
throat or background noise without getting confused. And that is basically what this new voice model

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:57.100
is doing when it decides every 0.4 seconds whether to speak or stay silent. It is running a constant

00:02:57.100 --> 00:03:03.760
lightweight check instead of one big end of message signal. So next time someone says real time voice

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.340
